      "writtenOn": "2021-06-08T21:34:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The retry conditions are a bit unclear. \n\nOld Kernel:\nr \u003c 0 \u0026\u0026 errno \u003d\u003d EAGAIN \u0026\u0026 result \u003d\u003d EC_RES_IN_PROGRESS\n\nNew Kernel:\nr \u003c 0 \u0026\u0026 errno \u003d\u003d EAGAIN \u0026\u0026 result \u003d\u003d EC_COMMAND_RESULT_UNINITIALIZED\n\nOr should we check errno \u003d\u003d EINPROGRESS on new kernel? Is it always safe to retry, if so loosening the retry condition should be ok.",
      "parentUuid": "e55bd686_a1e3e5ef",
      "revId": "c8af8d06b3082ca6730ed2039e55ab8f8ff6a873",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "49f68fbb_90376c8b",
        "filename": "util/comm-dev.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 187,
      "author": {
        "id": 1106949
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-07-06T16:55:12Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "hmm... I think there is a non-negligible chance of a race that the previous command result is actually from another process running somewhere on the system. Is the driver clever enough to distinguish EC_CMD_RESEND_RESPONSE by PID? If so, I agree it\u0027s benign to request a resend thus \u0027r\u003c0 \u0026\u0026 errno \u003d\u003d EAGAIN\u0027 should be good for both versions.\n\nIf not, I\u0027m not sure why we even use EC_CMD_RESEND_RESPONSE at all. (Thus, using it in more cases sounds a bad idea.)\n\nThere is no way to probe the driver version? Without it, I think whatever we do would generate obscure code. I think ample comment is highly appreciated.\n\nIf not, EINPROGRESS sounds like a solution.",
      "parentUuid": "3487c365_0ee8feb7",
      "revId": "c8af8d06b3082ca6730ed2039e55ab8f8ff6a873",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "360f5d82_dcceec4f",
        "filename": "util/comm-dev.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 187,
      "author": {
        "id": 1390635
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-07-19T17:56:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Looking at https://source.chromium.org/chromiumos/chromiumos/codesearch/+/main:src/platform/ec/common/host_command.c;l\u003d151 and crosbug.com/p/23806: Looks like overlapping HCs should be ignored. Also https://b.corp.google.com/issues/35528282#comment4 says \"we synchronize all commands through the kernel\" so in theory this should prevent overlapping host commands. \n\nI think https://source.chromium.org/chromiumos/chromiumos/codesearch/+/main:src/third_party/kernel/upstream/drivers/platform/chrome/cros_ec_proto.c;l\u003d213 should be updated to return EINPROGRESS.\n\nThen we can simplify the retry to be:\nr \u003c 0 \u0026\u0026 (result \u003d\u003d EC_RES_IN_PROGRESS || errno \u003d\u003d EINPROGRESS).",
      "parentUuid": "49f68fbb_90376c8b",
      "revId": "c8af8d06b3082ca6730ed2039e55ab8f8ff6a873",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7c747a5d_11dad6d8",
        "filename": "util/comm-dev.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 187,
      "author": {
        "id": 1106949
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-07-19T21:43:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The current implementation of the host command protocol doesn\u0027t seem prone to a race.\n\nIIUC, host_send_response (in host_commands.c) sets command_pending to 1 when a command handler returns IN_PROGRESS. A command handler (running in the HC task) later finishes a command and calls host_send_response (again), which sets saved_result. This is the value returned by EC_CMD_RESEND_RESPONSE.\n\nIf the host sends another command before RESEND_RESPONSE, the previous result is lost. So, I can\u0027t convince myself the current implementation is handling IN_PROGRESS in a race-free fashion.\n\nMaybe the kernel serializes host commands? That is, the kernel takes care of IN_PROGRESS (as done by send_command in cros_ec_proto.c). If so, we don\u0027t need to handle IN_PROGRESS in a user process, do we? Regardless, this seems a bad design since a slow command (e.g. flash erase) can block the channel. \n\nI see some code in the kernel and the EC, which blocks another command by using a status register (e.g. ec_response_timed_out using EC_LPC_STATUS_BUSY_MASK) but this is just arbitrating the bus access (at the physical layer). It doesn\u0027t help us avoid races around IN_PROGRESS.\n\n------------ \u003e8 ------------\nHow do I want to fix/change everything? I think we should discard RESEND_RESPONSE. Each command returning IN_PROGRESS should support some sort of GET_STATUS command as well. I don\u0027t think IN_PROGRESS can/should be handled at the common protocol layer.",
      "parentUuid": "360f5d82_dcceec4f",
      "revId": "c8af8d06b3082ca6730ed2039e55ab8f8ff6a873",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
